  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}+36y-16{x}^{2}+32x-124=0.$\n\n$y\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}+36y-16{x}^{2}+32x-124 & =0 \\\\ 9({y}^{2}+4y)-16({x}^{2}-2x)-124 & =0 \\\\ 9\\,[(y+2)^{2}-4]-16\\,[(x-1)^{2}-1]-124 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-36-16(x-1)^{2}+16-124 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-16(x-1)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{9(y+2)^{2}}{144}-\\frac{16(x-1)^{2}}{144} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(1,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =\\pm (\\frac{4}{3})(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\,\\frac{4}{3}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
